# SYNOPSIS

```usage
inspector
```

# DESCRIPTION

`inspector` shows every process proc(4) serves, which is every service and what each started, as a table: its name, its state, its share of the CPUs over the last second, its memory and its pid.
It is what ps(1) lists, kept current.
A click selects a row.
Its manifest makes it an `app` (manifest(6)), started from deskbar(4)'s menu.

A thread reads each `/proc/<pid>/status` once a second while someone is at the desktop, radharc(4)'s `idle` reading `active`.
While nobody is, it reads nothing and waits until someone is, and the window says it is paused.
A CPU share is the process's CPU time between two readings over the time between them, all the CPUs' time counting as one, so one process can show at most 100%; a process's first reading shows none.
Control-Q quits it.

Its manifest:

```text
service inspector
program inspector
app Inspector
export
use windows
mount /proc proc
mount /lib/fonts bootfs/lib/fonts
```

# FILES

: `/svc/inspector/status`
  Read only, a line each: `ready` once drawn; `processes <n>`; `desktop idle` or `desktop active`; `reads <n>`, how many times it has read /proc; `process <pid> <name>` for each process; and `wakeups <n>`, how many times its loop has woken.
: `/proc/<pid>/status`
  Each process's status line (proc(4)).

# ENVIRONMENT

: `INSPECTOR_TEST`
  Set, it logs `processes` and `desktop` as they change.

# SEE ALSO

proc(4), ps(1), preferences(4), deskbar(4), radharc(4), launcher(8)

# DIAGNOSTICS

It exits with 1, logging `inspector: FAILED:` and why, without /proc or when it can't reach Radharc.

# BUGS

It shows no jobs, threads, handles or channels between processes, and doesn't trace: proc(4) serves none of them.
The CPU share is user time alone, as proc(4) gives it.
A process can't be ended from it; write `kill` to its `/proc/<pid>/ctl`.
